PART A – PERSISTENCE DESIGN & PSEUDOCODE
TASK A1 – DESIGN A FILE FORMAT
Problem: Transport Office Bus-Pass Records

The university transport office issues approximately 8,000 bus passes every year. Each record contains seven fields: pass ID, student register number, name, route number, boarding stop, validity date and amount paid.

The system must support three operations:
1. Add a new bus-pass record.
2. Find a bus pass using its pass ID.
3. Display all bus passes belonging to a particular route.

A1.1 – Represent the Record 
Two file formats can be used to store bus-pass records: delimited text format and binary format.
(a) Delimited Text File Format
A delimited text file stores each bus-pass record as a single line. Each field is separated using a special delimiter such as the pipe symbol (|).

File name: buspasses.txt

Field order:
Field	Data type	Example
Pass ID	String	BP1001
Student Register Number	String	24CS001
Name	String	Arun Kumar
Route Number	Integer	12
Boarding Stop	String	Porur
Validity Date	Date (YYYY-MM-DD)	2027-03-31
Amount Paid	Decimal	4500.00

Record layout:
BP1001|24CS001|Arun Kumar|12|Porur|2027-03-31|4500.00
BP1002|24CS002|Priya Sharma|15|Guindy|2027-03-31|5000.00
BP1003|24CS003|Rahul Kumar|12|Porur|2027-03-31|4500.00


Handling names containing the delimiter:

If a student's name contains the delimiter (|), it can cause incorrect field separation.

To prevent this problem:
- Escape the delimiter using a backslash (\|).
- Escape an existing backslash as \\.
- Decode escaped characters while reading the file.
Alternatively, reject the delimiter in input fields or use a properly implemented CSV-style quoting format.

Advantages:
- Human-readable and easy to edit.
- Simple to create and maintain.
- Does not require special software to inspect.
- Suitable for storing approximately 8,000 records.
(b) Binary File Format Using DataOutputStream
A binary file stores records as bytes instead of readable text. Java's DataOutputStream writes primitive data types in a structured format.

File name: buspasses.dat

Field order and representation:
Field	Java data type	Storage method
Pass ID	String	writeUTF()
Student Register Number	String	writeUTF()
Name	String	writeUTF()
Route Number	Integer	writeInt()
Boarding Stop	String	writeUTF()
Validity Date	String	writeUTF()
Amount Paid	Double	writeDouble()

Example Java code for writing a record:
DataOutputStream dos = new DataOutputStream(
    new FileOutputStream("buspasses.dat", true)
);

dos.writeUTF("BP1001");
dos.writeUTF("24CS001");
dos.writeUTF("Arun Kumar");
dos.writeInt(12);
dos.writeUTF("Porur");
dos.writeUTF("2027-03-31");
dos.writeDouble(4500.00);

dos.close();


Advantages:
- Stores data in a compact binary representation.
- Preserves data types.
- Prevents delimiter-related problems.
- Suitable for structured records.
Limitation:
- Cannot be directly understood using a normal text editor.
- Requires DataInputStream or compatible classes to read the records.
Conclusion: The delimited text format is suitable when readability and easy maintenance are important, whereas the binary format is useful when structured storage and compact representation are preferred.

A1.2 – Design the Operations (3 Marks)
The text-file design uses Java character streams and buffered streams to perform the required operations.
1. Add a New Bus-Pass Record
Stream classes used:
- FileWriter – writes character data into the file.
- BufferedWriter – improves writing efficiency by reducing frequent disk operations.
Pseudocode: ADD_BUS_PASS
ALGORITHM ADD_BUS_PASS
BEGIN
    INPUT passID, registerNumber, name
    INPUT routeNumber, boardingStop
    INPUT validityDate, amountPaid

    VALIDATE all input fields

    IF any field is invalid THEN
        DISPLAY "Invalid bus-pass details"
        RETURN
    ENDIF

    OPEN buspasses.txt using FileWriter in append mode
    WRAP FileWriter using BufferedWriter

    WRITE escaped passID + "|"
    WRITE escaped registerNumber + "|"
    WRITE escaped name + "|"
    WRITE routeNumber + "|"
    WRITE escaped boardingStop + "|"
    WRITE validityDate + "|"
    WRITE amountPaid

    WRITE newline

    FLUSH BufferedWriter
    CLOSE BufferedWriter

    DISPLAY "Bus pass added successfully"
END


Explanation:

The file is opened in append mode so that existing records are not overwritten. BufferedWriter improves performance by collecting characters in memory before writing them to the file.

A production implementation should also check that the pass ID is unique before appending.
2. Find a Bus Pass by Pass ID
Stream classes used:
- FileReader – reads character data from the file.
- BufferedReader – reads the file line by line efficiently.
Pseudocode: FIND_BUS_PASS
ALGORITHM FIND_BUS_PASS
BEGIN
    INPUT searchID

    IF buspasses.txt does not exist THEN
        DISPLAY "Bus-pass file not found"
        RETURN
    ENDIF

    OPEN buspasses.txt using FileReader
    WRAP FileReader using BufferedReader

    found ← FALSE

    WHILE a line can be read DO

        SPLIT line using escaped delimiter parser

        IF number of fields is not 7 THEN
            DISPLAY "Damaged record skipped"
            CONTINUE
        ENDIF

        IF field[0] = searchID THEN
            DISPLAY all fields of the record
            found ← TRUE
            EXIT LOOP
        ENDIF

    ENDWHILE

    CLOSE BufferedReader

    IF found = FALSE THEN
        DISPLAY "Bus pass not found"
    ENDIF
END


Explanation:

The program reads each record sequentially and compares its pass ID with the requested ID. If a match is found, the record is displayed.

BufferedReader is used because it provides efficient line-by-line reading.
3. List All Bus Passes on a Given Route
Stream classes used:
- FileReader – reads character data.
- BufferedReader – reads records line by line.
Pseudocode: LIST_PASSES_BY_ROUTE
ALGORITHM LIST_PASSES_BY_ROUTE
BEGIN
    INPUT searchRoute

    IF buspasses.txt does not exist THEN
        DISPLAY "Bus-pass file not found"
        RETURN
    ENDIF

    OPEN buspasses.txt using FileReader
    WRAP FileReader using BufferedReader

    found ← FALSE

    WHILE a line can be read DO

        SPLIT line using escaped delimiter parser

        IF number of fields is not 7 THEN
            DISPLAY "Damaged record skipped"
            CONTINUE
        ENDIF

        IF field[3] = searchRoute THEN
            DISPLAY all fields of the record
            found ← TRUE
        ENDIF

    ENDWHILE

    CLOSE BufferedReader

    IF found = FALSE THEN
        DISPLAY "No bus passes found for this route"
    ENDIF
END


Explanation:

The program scans every record and compares its route number with the given route number. All matching records are displayed.
Justification of Stream Classes
Stream class	Purpose	Reason for selection
FileReader	Read text files	Reads character data
FileWriter	Write text files	Writes character data
BufferedReader	Read records	Supports efficient line-by-line reading
BufferedWriter	Write records	Reduces frequent disk operations
FileInputStream	Read binary files	Reads raw bytes
FileOutputStream	Write binary files	Writes raw bytes

FileInputStream and FileOutputStream are suitable for binary file operations, whereas FileReader, FileWriter and their buffered versions are appropriate for the delimited text-file design.

A1.3 – Design for Damaged Data (2 Marks)
The system must handle damaged records, missing files and incomplete writes safely.
1. Handling a Damaged Line
A damaged line may contain:
- Missing fields.
- Extra fields.
- Invalid route numbers.
- Incorrect date formats.
- Invalid amount values.
- Incorrect delimiter escaping.
Solution:
- Split the line using a proper escape-aware parser.
- Check whether exactly seven fields are available.
- Validate the data types and date format.
- Skip invalid records instead of terminating the entire program.
- Display an error message and record the affected line number in an error report.
- Never overwrite the original file automatically when damaged records are encountered.
Example:
BP1004|24CS004|Kiran|12|Porur|4500.00


This record is damaged because it contains only six fields instead of seven.

The program should skip this record and continue reading the remaining records.
2. Handling a Missing File
If buspasses.txt does not exist, the program should not terminate unexpectedly.

Solution:
- Check whether the file exists before reading.
- Display an appropriate error message.
- When adding the first record, create the file automatically.
- Do not silently treat a missing file as an empty database when performing a search.
Example Java code:
File file = new File("buspasses.txt");

if (!file.exists()) {
    System.out.println("Bus-pass file not found");
}


3. Handling an Incomplete Write Due to Power Failure
A power failure during writing may leave a partially written record in the file.

Solution:
1. Write new records to a temporary file first.
2. Flush buffered data to the underlying stream.
3. Use FileDescriptor.sync() when stronger disk-write guarantees are required.
4. Validate the temporary file.
5. Replace the original file with the temporary file using an atomic move where supported.
6. Retain a backup of the previous valid file.
For append-based writing, use a journal or write-ahead log with record lengths or checksums so incomplete records can be detected and recovered.

Example:
ALGORITHM SAFE_FILE_UPDATE
BEGIN
    CREATE temporary file

    COPY existing valid records into temporary file
    WRITE new record into temporary file

    FLUSH all buffered data
    SYNCHRONIZE file data to disk

    VALIDATE temporary file

    IF validation is successful THEN
        ATOMICALLY REPLACE original file
    ELSE
        DELETE temporary file
        DISPLAY "Update failed"
    ENDIF
END


Conclusion:

Data validation, exception handling, backups and temporary-file replacement help prevent data corruption and improve the reliability of the persistence system.

Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
Alternative: RandomAccessFile with Fixed-Length Records
Instead of storing records in a delimited text file, the university can use Java's RandomAccessFile to store records at fixed positions.

Each record occupies exactly the same number of bytes.

For example, the following fixed-length layout can be used:
Field	Storage size
Pass ID	10 bytes
Register Number	15 bytes
Name	40 bytes
Route Number	4 bytes
Boarding Stop	30 bytes
Validity Date	10 bytes
Amount Paid	8 bytes
Total	117 bytes

The string fields are padded with spaces or other agreed padding bytes when their actual length is smaller than the allocated size. UTF-8 strings must be encoded and padded carefully to ensure exact byte lengths.

Finding a record by position:

If the record index is known, its location is calculated using:
\[
\text{Position} = i \times \text{recordSize}
\]

For example, to access record number 5 (zero-based index 4):
\[
4 \times 117 = 468 \text{ bytes}
\]

The file pointer moves directly to byte position 468.

Example Java code:
RandomAccessFile raf =
    new RandomAccessFile("buspasses.dat", "rw");

int recordSize = 117;
int recordIndex = 4;

raf.seek((long) recordIndex * recordSize);

byte[] passID = new byte[10];
raf.readFully(passID);

System.out.println(
    new String(passID).trim()
);

raf.close();


Important: This approach provides O(1) access when the record index is already known. Finding a pass using only its pass ID still requires an O(n) scan unless an additional index mapping pass IDs to record positions is maintained.
Complexity Comparison
Let:
- \(n\) = total number of bus-pass records.
- \(R\) = fixed record size in bytes.
Operation	Delimited Text File	RandomAccessFile
Add a record	O(1) amortized append	O(1) append
Find by pass ID	O(n)	O(n) without an index
Find by known record index	O(n) sequential access	O(1) seek
List records by route	O(n)	O(n)
Space complexity	O(n)	O(n × R)

Space Wastage in Fixed-Length Records
Fixed-length records allocate a fixed amount of storage even when the actual data is smaller.

For example:
- Allocated name field size = 40 bytes.
- Actual name size = 10 bytes.
- Wasted space = 30 bytes.
For 8,000 records, if every name uses only 10 bytes:
\[
8000 \times 30 = 240000 \text{ bytes}
\]

Therefore, approximately 240 KB is wasted just in the name fields.

Other fixed-length fields may also waste space.
Advantages of RandomAccessFile
1. Provides direct access to records when their positions are known.
2. Avoids scanning the entire file for position-based retrieval.
3. Supports updating records at specific locations.
4. Suitable for applications requiring frequent random access.
Disadvantages of RandomAccessFile
1. Fixed-length fields can waste storage space.
2. Requires careful record-position calculations.
3. Changing the record layout can be difficult.
4. Finding a record by ID still requires scanning without an index.
Final Comparison and Decision
For the university transport office, which manages approximately 8,000 bus-pass records annually, a delimited text file is a practical choice because it is readable, simple to maintain and easy to inspect.

However, if the number of records increases significantly and the application frequently retrieves or updates records using known positions, RandomAccessFile can offer better performance.

Final decision: I would select the delimited text-file approach for the initial implementation because it provides simplicity, readability and sufficient performance for the given requirements. If faster pass-ID searches become necessary, I would introduce a separate index mapping pass IDs to record positions or migrate to a database.
